{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a Crusader lord who ruled as the Count of Tripoli from around 1175 until his death in 1187. Born likely in the early 1140s he was the youngest son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierne of Rethel. Raymond III’s early life is not well documented but he became involved in the political and military affairs of the Crusader states following the death of his father in 1152.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Early Career and Succession*\nRaymond III’s rise to prominence began after his elder brother Humphrey IV died in 1173 without legitimate heirs. This left Raymond as the sole heir to Tripoli despite the presence of other male relatives. The succession was contested by Bohemond III of Antioch who claimed a share in Tripoli through his mother Alice of Antioch who was Raymond II’s daughter. Raymond III successfully resisted Bohemond’s claims and secured his position as count with the support of the Templars and Hospitallers. His reign started under the regency of his mother Hodierne until he came of age and took full control.\n\nDuring his rule Raymond III navigated the complex geopolitics of the Levant where Crusader states were under constant pressure from Muslim powers. He maintained alliances with other Crusader lords but his relationship with Baldwin IV of Jerusalem was often strained. Baldwin was afflicted with leprosy and his inability to lead effectively created division among the Crusaders. Raymond’s relationships with other leaders like Reynald of Chatillon and Guy of Lusignan also fluctuated due to personal and political conflicts.\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Military Campaigns and Diplomacy*\nRaymond III participated in several military campaigns to defend the Crusader states against the expanding forces of Saladin. In 1176 he joined Baldwin IV in a campaign against Aleppo but the expedition was a failure due to internal divisions among the Crusaders. His most notable engagement was the Battle of La Forbie in 1179 where a combined Crusader army was ambushed and decisively defeated by Saladin’s forces. Raymond III survived the battle but lost a significant portion of his army and prestige.\n\nDespite military setbacks Raymond III focused on diplomacy to secure the survival of Tripoli. He maintained relations with both the Crusaders and Muslim leaders including Saladin himself. In 1183 he sent his son Bohemond as a hostage to Saladin to negotiate a truce that temporarily eased tensions. This diplomatic effort highlighted Raymond’s pragmatic approach to governance.\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Later Years and Death*\nIn the later years of his rule Raymond III faced increasing pressure from Saladin’s growing power. The fall of Jerusalem in 1187 to Saladin’s forces marked a turning point in the Crusader states’ fortunes. Raymond III died later that same year in 1187 leaving his son and namesake Raymond IV as his heir. His death occurred during a period of significant turmoil for the Crusader states which were on the verge of collapse in the face of Saladin’s military campaigns.\n\nRaymond III’s legacy is often overshadowed by the dramatic events of the Third Crusade and the broader struggle against Saladin. However his efforts to maintain Tripoli’s independence through diplomacy and cautious military engagement were crucial in prolonging the existence of the Crusader states in the region. His reign reflected the challenges faced by Crusader leaders who had to balance internal divisions with the external threats posed by powerful Muslim enemies.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.06853077560663223}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.00014161060971673578}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0053452192805707455}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.2384123057126999}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00038389943074434996}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.0184103